## What happened

Starting with .NET 7, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS**. Apple removed DSA from its cryptographic frameworks, and .NET no longer ships a managed fallback for it on that platform. If your .NET 10 builds worked, you were likely using a workaround or an older runtime patch — .NET 11 enforces this consistently.

## How to fix it

You have a few options:

1. **Use a cross-platform managed library** — replace the built-in `DSA` class with [BouncyCastle](https://www.nuget.org/packages/BouncyCastle.Cryptography) (`Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto`), which implements DSA in pure managed code and works on all platforms.

2. **Verify signatures on Linux/Windows only** — if macOS doesn't need to verify at runtime, guard with a platform check:
   ```csharp
   if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
   {
       using var dsa = DSA.Create();
       // ...
   }
   ```

3. **Migrate away from DSA entirely** — DSA is considered legacy. If you control the signing side, re-sign with **ECDSA** or **Ed25519** (supported natively on all platforms in modern .NET).

## Recommended path

If you must continue verifying existing DSA signatures on all three platforms, use BouncyCastle:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKeyParameters);
verifier.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
bool valid = verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
```

This gives you a single code path across Linux, Windows, and macOS with no platform-specific exceptions.
